    "source": {
      "provider": "nanoka",
      "endpoint": "https://static.nanoka.cc/gi/6.7.54/en/character/10000150.json",
      "note": "Odette is not in Genshin Optimizer yet, so hit keys follow this repo's recent-character convention (hit1Dmg.. + chargedDmg + plunge*Dmg, with the Stellar variants suffixed *StellarDmg as in sandrone.json) and may need renaming once GO adds her. Melee sword, so NA/CA/plunge are physical.\n\nIMPORTANT — 'Stellar Glimmer' is the umbrella term for BOTH Stellar-Conduct and Stellar Swirl (see the in-game glossary entry Radiance: Stellar Glimmer), so the DMG% bucket is split in two: buffs the kit words 'Stellar Glimmer' use stellarglimmer_dmg_ (reaches either reaction), buffs worded 'Stellar-Conduct' use stellarconduct_dmg_. Every one of Odette's buffs below is Glimmer-worded, so they all use the umbrella. Her *StellarDmg hit definitions are the Stellar-CONDUCT variants and carry BOTH keys, so both buckets reach them. The Stellar Swirl multipliers are recorded above as *StellarSwirlDmg_mult with no hit definition: Stellar Swirl DMG is still unimplemented (its base multiplier, EM curve and DEF behaviour are unpublished — it spawns a levelling Stellar Vortex rather than resolving instantly, so Stellar-Conduct's constants cannot be reused). Adding it means new reaction constants in the evaluator, not just seed data.\n\nA4 Pathetique (Stellar Glimmer DMG +1.5% per 100 ATK above 1,000, cap +30%) is an UNCONDITIONAL self modifier using the stellarglimmer_dmg_from_atk_ / _cap_ conversion (STAT_CONVERSION_SPECS, source_offset=1000), so it reads the build's own live ATK — post-buff, and after the ATK conversions above it. It used to be three hand-picked admin states; do not re-add those, they would double-count.\n\nSTILL NOT MODELLED: passive3's base-DMG term (+0.7% per 100 ATK, cap +14%) is the same shape but it is a TEAM buff on reactions a party member triggers, scaled by ODETTE's ATK — a conversion would read the wrong character's ATK whenever she is a teammate rather than the main, so it stays a fixed-state condition. C2's 20% elemental RES shred is included; C6's 'no longer decreases' clause is an uptime statement, not a stat.",
      "assumptions": {
        "constellation": 0,
        "characterLevel": 90,
        "autoTalentLevel": 10,
        "skillTalentLevel": 10,
        "burstTalentLevel": 10
      }
    }
